 "commits": [
  {
   "sha": "20c0018e8cbcc7586f5fe5b7d55f3cb3c15f75f8",
   "node_id": "C_kwDOVNfIN9oAKDIwYzAwMThlOGNiY2M3NTg2ZjVmZTViN2Q1NWYzY2IzYzE1Zjc1Zjg",
   "commit": {
    "author": {
     "name": "Ethan Resnick",
     "email": "ethan.resnick@gmail.com",
     "date": "2026-10-19T15:09:00Z"
    },
    "committer": {
     "name": "Ethan Resnick",
     "email": "ethan.resnick@gmail.com",
     "date": "2026-10-19T15:09:00Z"
    },
    "message": "MINOR: Pass the configured delay to the echo server in SaslAuthenticatorFailureDelayTest\n\nSaslAuthenticatorFailurePositiveDelayTest sets failedAuthenticationDelayMs\nto 200, and the teardown asserts that at least that much mock time passed.\nBut createEchoServer calls the NetworkTestUtils overload without a delay,\nwhich uses a fixed 100 ms. So the server never applies the configured\ndelay. The assertion passes only because the test reads the MockTime(1)\nclock enough extra times before the teardown, and each read advances it\nby 1 ms. SaslAuthenticatorFailureNoDelayTest (delay 0) also gets the\n100 ms server delay.\n\nPass failedAuthenticationDelayMs to the overload that takes a delay, so\nthe server applies the delay that each subclass configures.\n\nTesting: all 8 tests in SaslAuthenticatorFailurePositiveDelayTest and\nSaslAuthenticatorFailureNoDelayTest pass on trunk with OpenJDK 21. We\nfound the problem by running these tests on a runtime that reads the\nclock fewer times on the same path (Java compiled to WebAssembly). There,\ntestInvalidPasswordSaslPlain, testInvalidPasswordSaslScram, and\ntestDisabledSaslMechanism failed with \"timeSpent: 193\" against the\nexpected 200.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
    "tree": {
     "sha": "48c933b12bf5de7479f3e9661e0b7a97ac25ae64",
     "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/apache/kafka/git/trees/48c933b12bf5de7479f3e9661e0b7a97ac25ae64"
